Here's the diagram of right triangle ABC with an inscribed circle, labeled as requested:

```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=10pt]{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Define the coordinates of the triangle vertices
    \coordinate (A) at (0,3);
    \coordinate (B) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (4,0);

    % Draw the right triangle ABC
    \draw[thick, line join=round] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- cycle;

    % Mark the right angle at B
    \pic [draw, "$90^\circ$", angle radius=10mm, angle eccentricity=1.2] {angle = C--B--A};

    % Calculate the inradius and the coordinates of the incenter
    \pgfmathsetmacro{\inradius}{sqrt(3)*3/2-1.5}
    \coordinate (I) at (1.5, \inradius);

    % Draw the inscribed circle
    \draw[thick] (I) circle (\inradius);

    % Calculate the touch points
    \coordinate (D) at (0, \inradius);
    \coordinate (E) at (1.5, 0);
    \coordinate (F) at ({4 - \inradius*4/3}, {3 - \inradius*3/3});

    % Draw the lines from the incenter to the touch points
    \draw[thick] (I) -- (D);
    \draw[thick] (I) -- (E);
    \draw[thick] (I) -- (F);

    % Label the vertices
    \node[anchor=south] at (A) {A};
    \node[anchor=north] at (B) {B};
    \node[anchor=north] at (C) {C};

    % Label the incenter
    \node[anchor=south] at (I) {I};

    % Label the touch points
    \node[anchor=south] at (D) {D};
    \node[anchor=west] at (E) {E};
    \node[anchor=south] at (F) {F};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This diagram shows:
- Right triangle ABC with AB = 3 and BC = 4
- The right angle at B is marked with a 90° symbol
- The inscribed circle with center I
- The touch points D, E, and F where the circle touches the sides of the triangle
- All labels are anchored away from the vertices for clarity

The triangle follows the Pythagorean theorem since $3^2 + 4^2 = 5^2$ (AB² + BC² = AC²). The inradius (radius of the inscribed circle) is calculated based on the triangle's area and semi-perimeter.

Here's the normal distribution curve with the requested shading and labels:

```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=10pt]{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Draw the normal distribution curve
    \draw[blue, thick, domain=-3:3, samples=100] plot (\x, {exp(-(\x)^2/2)});

    % Shade the area between mu-2sigma and mu+2sigma
    \fill[blue!10] (-2,0) -- plot[domain=-2:2] (\x, {exp(-(\x)^2/2)}) -- (2,0) -- cycle;

    % Draw the x-axis
    \draw[->] (-3,0) -- (3,0) node[right] {$x$};

    % Draw the y-axis
    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0,1.2) node[above] {};

    % Label mu, mu-sigma, mu+sigma, mu-2sigma, and mu+2sigma
    \node[anchor=north] at (0,0) {$\mu$};
    \node[anchor=north] at (-1,0) {$\mu-\sigma$};
    \node[anchor=north] at (1,0) {$\mu+\sigma$};
    \node[anchor=north] at (-2,0) {$\mu-2\sigma$};
    \node[anchor=north] at (2,0) {$\mu+2\sigma$};

    % Add ticks to the x-axis
    \foreach \x in {-2,-1,0,1,2} {
        \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05);
    }
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This diagram shows:
- A normal distribution curve centered at $\mu$ (mu)
- The area between $\mu-2\sigma$ and $\mu+2\sigma$ shaded in light blue
- Labels for $\mu$, $\mu-\sigma$, $\mu+\sigma$, $\mu-2\sigma$, and $\mu+2\sigma$ on the x-axis
- The x-axis is scaled to 1.5 times the standard size to ensure labels don't clump

The shaded area represents approximately 95% of the data in a normal distribution, which is a key concept in statistics.

Here's the diagram of a block on a 30-degree inclined plane with the requested force vectors:

```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=10pt]{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Define the coordinates for the inclined plane
    \coordinate (A) at (0,0);
    \coordinate (B) at (4,0);
    \coordinate (C) at (4,1.5);
    \coordinate (D) at (0,1.5);

    % Draw the inclined plane
    \draw[thick] (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;

    % Draw the block on the inclined plane
    \draw[fill=gray!50] (1,0) rectangle (2,0.75);

    % Draw the gravity vector (F_g)
    \draw[->, ultra thick, red] (1.5,0.375) -- (1.5,-0.5) node[anchor=north] {$\mathbf{F_g}$};

    % Draw the normal force vector (F_n)
    \draw[->, ultra thick, blue] (1.5,0.375) -- (1.5+0.5*cos(30),0.375+0.5*sin(30)) node[anchor=west] {$\mathbf{F_n}$};

    % Draw the friction force vector (F_f)
    \draw[->, ultra thick, green] (1.5,0.375) -- (1.5-0.3*cos(60),0.375-0.3*sin(60)) node[anchor=north] {$\mathbf{F_f}$};

    % Add angle indicator for the inclined plane
    \draw[->] (0,0) -- (0.5,0) arc (0:30:0.5) node[anchor=west] {$30^\circ$};

    % Add labels to the vectors
    \node[anchor=south] at (1.5,-0.5) {Gravity};
    \node[anchor=west] at (1.5+0.5*cos(30),0.375+0.5*sin(30)) {Normal Force};
    \node[anchor=north] at (1.5-0.3*cos(60),0.375-0.3*sin(60)) {Friction};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This diagram shows:
- A block on a 30-degree inclined plane
- Three force vectors:
  - Gravity (F_g) pointing downward
  - Normal Force (F_n) perpendicular to the plane
  - Friction (F_f) opposing the motion along the plane
- All labels are anchored away from the vectors for clarity

The vectors represent the forces acting on an object placed on an inclined plane, which is a fundamental concept in physics when studying forces and motion.

Here's the plot of the function $f(x) = \frac{1}{x-2}$ with the requested asymptotes and labels:

```tikz
\documentclass[tikz,border=10pt]{standalone}
\usepackage{tikz}
\usetikzlibrary{positioning, calc, angles, quotes}
\tikzset{every node/.style={font=\footnotesize, inner sep=2pt, outer sep=0pt}, node distance=10mm and 10mm}

\begin{document}
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.5, transform shape]
    % Draw the function f(x) = 1/(x-2)
    \draw[blue, thick, domain=0:4, samples=100] plot (\x, {1/(\x-2)});

    % Draw the vertical asymptote at x=2
    \draw[red, dashed] (2, -1) -- (2, 3) node[anchor=west] {Vertical Asymptote $x=2$};

    % Draw the horizontal asymptote at y=0
    \draw[red, dashed] (-1, 0) -- (4, 0) node[anchor=south east] {Horizontal Asymptote $y=0$};

    % Draw the x-axis
    \draw[->] (-1,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};

    % Draw the y-axis
    \draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,3) node[above] {$y$};

    % Label the intercept
    \fill[black] (1,1) circle (2.5pt);
    \node[anchor=south east] at (1,1) {Intercept $(1,1)$};

    % Add ticks to the axes
    \foreach \x in {1,2,3} {
        \draw (\x,0.05) -- (\x,-0.05) node[below] {\x};
    }
    \foreach \y in {1,2} {
        \draw (0.05,\y) -- (-0.05,\y) node[left] {\y};
    }
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{document}
```

This diagram shows:
- The function $f(x) = \frac{1}{x-2}$ plotted in blue
- A vertical asymptote at $x=2$ (red dashed line)
- A horizontal asymptote at $y=0$ (red dashed line)
- The intercept point at $(1,1)$ marked with a black dot
- All labels are anchored away from the plot for clarity

The vertical asymptote occurs where the denominator is zero ($x=2$), and the horizontal asymptote is at $y=0$ because as $x$ approaches infinity, the function approaches 0. The intercept is where the function crosses the y-axis, which occurs when $x=1$.

